#: ../../source/guide/graph_structures/adjacency.rst:12
#: b30b7320f9cc46c1b256d6c81cd7a633
msgid ""
"**Adjacency representations** are fundamental ways to store graph data in"
" memory. The two main approaches—**adjacency lists** and **adjacency "
"matrices**—offer different trade-offs between space efficiency and "
"operation speed. Understanding when to use each representation is crucial"
" for building efficient graph algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guide/graph_structures/adjacency.rst:38
#: 36be1dc5c1084ccf911042126a13cf05
msgid ""
"Adjacency lists are optimal for **sparse graphs** (few edges), while "
"adjacency matrices excel with **dense graphs** (many edges). Most real-"
"world graphs are sparse, making lists the default choice."
msgstr ""

